<mxfile host="Electron" type="device" version="24.7.17" agent="NHSBSA" modified="2026-09-11T15:45:00Z" author="Seamus Sands" organisation="NHSBSA">
  <diagram id="01-journey-analyst" name="01-journey-analyst" description="Analyst: journey through the playbook. Stops run across the top in the order they are met, and this reads one stop at a time. Nine steps, from the question you were handed to the lessons you record. Stop 1: You have been asked to do a piece of analysis. In the playbook: Check the playbook applies. What you do there: Check it applies, then start at Phase 1. Outputs: Nothing recorded yet. Watch out for: None. Stop 2: Is this the question they meant to ask? In the playbook: Phase 1 Engagement and scoping. What you do there: Agree the decision, the question, what is out of scope, the deadline. Outputs: The scope and specification, with the question in one sentence. Watch out for: Challenging a brief is part of the job. Stop 3: How much assurance does this need? In the playbook: Deciding your assurance level. What you do there: Score the impact with your commissioner at scoping. Score the likelihood at design, or at scoping if the work is Business Critical. Outputs: The Analytical Assurance Decision Tool, with the Business Critical answer either way. Watch out for: The impact at scoping and not later. About five minutes. Stop 4: What do I actually have to do? In the playbook: The checks, by level. What you do there: Read the block for your level and the blocks inside it. Outputs: The quality assurance checklist, and your reviewer's time in the schedule. Watch out for: The checks are cumulative. Name your reviewer before you start. Stop 5: What do I not know, and how much does it matter? In the playbook: R10 Uncertainty. What you do there: Find the principal sources of uncertainty while design can still change. Outputs: Assumptions listed with their rationale and the impact if wrong. Watch out for: No agreed margin of error means no threshold to test against. Stop 6: Someone independent needs to check this. In the playbook: Phase 3 Analysis. What you do there: Your own verification and validation first, then the critical friend. Outputs: The quality assurance log, and what you did about each finding. Watch out for: A checklist shows intent. A log shows it was done. Stop 7: How do I write this up without overclaiming? In the playbook: Phase 4 Delivery and sign-off. What you do there: State the caveats in the output itself, not only in an annex. Outputs: The output and the quality assurance statement, published from Level 3. Watch out for: None. Stop 8: Is any of this ethically or legally loaded? In the playbook: R11 Data ethics and personal data. What you do there: Check the Data and AI Ethics Framework, personal data and transparency scope. Outputs: The ethics position recorded, and a transparency record drafted where in scope. Watch out for: Ask information governance before the work starts, not at sign-off. Stop 9: It is out. Now what? In the playbook: Phase 5 Review. What you do there: Ask whether it worked, and whether it is still right. Outputs: The review record, the lessons, and any decommissioning decision. Watch out for: At Level 1 the review is yours to own. The one branch, after step 3: Any one impact dimension at 4? Yes. The work is Business Critical. Business Critical analysis is the route in nine steps, and it brings Level 4 BCA, a register entry and a named Analysis Senior Responsible Officer, all starting at scoping. Everything else on this journey still applies. The loop: Step 9 loops back to step 2. What you record at the end is what the next piece of work starts from. Open alongside, at any step: R8 for which standards bind this work. Roles and responsibilities for who owns a decision you are stuck on. R2 for the template. R3 for the person. NHSBSA Producing Quality Analysis Playbook. Service design artefacts for workshop use. Seamus Sands for NHSBSA, SOW-DOC053.">
